===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q7873}} A [[vital]] [[liquid]] flowing in the [[bodies]] of many types of [[animals]] that usually conveys [[nutrient]]s and [[oxygen]]. In vertebrates, it is colored [[red]] by [[hemoglobin]], is conveyed by [[artery|arteries]] and [[vein]]s, is [[pumped]] by the [[heart]] and is usually generated in [[bone]] [[marrow]].
#: {{ux|en|Some insects are known for consuming '''blood'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|chapter=Of the Cameleon|page=133|passage=It cannot be denied it [the [[chameleon]]] is (if not the moſt of any) a very abſtemious animall, and ſuch as by reaſon of its frigidity, paucity of '''bloud''', and latitancy in the winter (about which time the obſervations are often made) will long ſubſist without a viſible ſuſtentation.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1927|author=[http://openlibrary.org/authors/OL2416183A F. E. Penny]| chapter=4| title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL16814587W Pulling the Strings]| passage=The case was that of a murder. It had an element of mystery about it, however, which was puzzling the authorities. A turban and loincloth soaked in '''blood''' had been found; also a staff.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=A better waterworks| date=2013-06-01| volume=407| issue=8838| page=5 (Technology Quarterly)| magazine=w:The Economist| url=http://www.economist.com/news/technology-quarterly/21578526-medical-technology-artificial-kidneys-are-getting-closer-becoming-clinical| passage=An artificial kidney these days still means a refrigerator-sized dialysis machine. Such devices mimic the way real kidneys cleanse '''blood''' and eject impurities and surplus water as urine.}}
# A [[family]] [[relationship]] due to [[birth]], such as that between [[sibling]]s; contrasted with relationships due to [[marriage]] or [[adoption]]. See {{m|en|blood relative}}, {{m|en|blood relation}}.
#: {{syn|en|background|descent|heritage|stock}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1690|author=w:Edmund Waller|title=The Maid's Tragedy Altered
|passage=a [[friend]] of our own '''blood'''}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe|passage=to [[share]] the '''blood''' of Saxon royalty}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} One of the four [[humour]]s in the human body.
# {{rfv-sense|en}} The [[endometrial]] lining as it is shed in [[menstruation]]; [[menstrual fluid]].
# {{lb|en|medicine|countable}} A [[blood test]] or [[blood sample]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Steve Jamieson|title=Bilbo the Lifeguard Dog|passage=When I got Bilbo to their surgery the vet took Bilbo in for tests. {{...}} His '''bloods''' showed nothing wrong at all.}}
# The [[sap]] or [[juice]] which [[flows]] in or from [[plant]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1841|author=Benjamin Parsons|title=Anti-Bacchus|url=http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/65270457|page=95
|passage=It is no tautology to call the '''blood''' of the grape red or purple, because the juice of that fruit was sometimes white and sometimes black or dark. The arterial blood of our bodies is red, but the venous is called "black blood."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1901|author=Levi Leslie Lamborn|title=American Carnation Culture|section=fourth edition, page 57
|passage=[[disbudding|Disbudding]] is merely a species of pruning, and should be done as soon as the lateral buds begin to develop on the cane. It diverts the flow of the plant's '''blood''' from many buds into one or a few, thus increasing the size of the flower, [...]}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1916|author=John Gordon Dorrance|title=The Story of the Forest|page=44
|passage=Look at a leaf. On it are many little raised lines which reach out to all parts of the leaf and back to the stem and twig. These are "veins," full of the tree's '''blood'''. It is white and looks very much like water; [...]}}
# {{lb|en|poetic}} The juice of anything, especially if red.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Genesis|49|11|passage=He washed{{...}}his clothes in the '''blood''' of grapes.}}
# [[temper|Temper]] of [[mind]]; [[disposition]]; [[mood]]
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|act=4|scene=iii|passage=When thou perceive his '''blood''' inclined to mirth}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=Sheridan Le Fanu| title=w:The House by the Churchyard| passage=There was some little undefinable coolness between old General Chattesworth and Devereux. He admired the young fellow, and he liked good '''blood''' in his corps, but somehow he was glad when he thought he was likely to go. When old Bligh, of the Magazine, commended the handsome young dog's good looks, the general would grow grave all at once {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A lively, [[showy]] [[man]]; a [[rake]]; a [[dandy]].
#: {{syn|en|fop|popinjay|Thesaurus:dandy}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|III|iii|passage=Seest thou not{{...}}how giddily 'a turns about all the hot '''bloods''' between fourteen and five and thirty?}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|3|text={{...}} it was the morning costume of a dandy or '''blood''' of those days {{...}}}}
# A [[blood horse]], one of good pedigree.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[bloodshed|Bloodshed]].
#: {{ux|en|They came looking for '''blood'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Cato|i|1|Our Father’s Death<br>Would fill up all the Guilt of Civil War,<br>And cloſe the Scene of '''Blood'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1873| title=The Monthly Packet of Evening Readings for Members of the English Church| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=L2sJAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA31| page=31| 1=en| passage=Under Henry III. Amboise ceased to be a slaughter-house, as in the preceding reign, but it remained a sort of state prison. It is related that Anne d'Este of Ferrara, wife of Duc de Guise, while assisting once at a series of executions out of the windows of the castle with Catherine de Medicis, suddenly overcome by the horror of the spectacle, turned away, exclaiming passionately, "Ah Madame! how all this '''blood''' calls out for '''blood'''! what vengeance is being prepared! May God have pity on your sons and on mine!"}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2007|author=Christine Feehan| title=Deadly Game| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ELE3WO9-mNMC&pg=PA55| isbn=9781101146972| page=55| publisher=Penguin| 1=en| passage=He watched out for the men in his unit, for the one woman who had saved them so many years ago when they were still raw teens out for '''blood''' and revenge on the world, and he watched out for anyone else they stumbled across in their lives that needed protection.}}
#* {{quote-book| year=2010| author=Alison Futrell |title=Blood in the Arena: The Spectacle of Roman Power
|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VRG1m8LjUloC&pg=PA169| isbn=9780292792401| page=169| publisher=University of Texas Press| 1=en| passage=The standard assessment suggests that as the munera became purely a spectacle, they became more murderous because the public wanted to see '''blood'''. That the people of Rome were able to indulge this degenerate desire was merely due to the degraded status of the professional gladiator.}}
# {{lb|en|especially|_|AAVE}} A friend or acquaintance, especially one who is black and male.
# {{alt case form of|en|Blood||member of a certain gang}}
# {{lb|en|UK|MLE|slang}} {{alt form|en|blud||Informal address to a male.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=Joseph Barnes Phillips|title=Big Foot ...and Tiny Little Heartstrings
|passage='''Blood''' I swear she just gave man extra chicken? Two fat pieces of chicken.}}

